<template>
  <main class="page shell">
    <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/journal">Journal</a> / Tempering</p>
    <article class="prose">
      <header>
        <p class="eyebrow">Workshop note</p>
        <h1>Tempering by hand, and why we still do it</h1>
        <p class="muted">
          <time datetime="2026-09-18">September 18, 2026</time> · 5 min read
        </p>
      </header>

      <p>
        Tempering is the difference between chocolate that snaps and chocolate that
        bends like a fruit leather. It is also the step most likely to go wrong at
        7am, before the first coffee.
      </p>

      <p>
        Cocoa butter can crystallize in six forms. Only form V gives you the gloss and
        the clean break we want. The trick is to melt everything, cool it to form IV,
        then warm it just enough to convert to V without going back.
      </p>

      <h2>The window</h2>
      <p>
        Dark chocolate wants to be worked at 31°C. Milk and white are more forgiving at
        29°C. A degree too warm and the bar blooms in the wrapper a week later; a
        degree too cool and it sets streaky.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>Melt to 45°C to erase all memory of previous crystals.</li>
        <li>Seed with tempered chocolate and stir down to 27°C.</li>
        <li>Bring back up to 31°C and hold it there.</li>
      </ul>

      <blockquote>
        “If the thermometer is off by two degrees, the batch is off by two degrees.
        Calibrate the thermometer, not your patience.”
      </blockquote>

      <h2>Why not a machine?</h2>
      <p>
        We have a tempering machine. It lives under the counter, and it is wonderful
        for production runs of the house bar. The hand method is how we test new
        origins: two kilos at a time, on a marble slab, with a spatula and a laser
        thermometer.
      </p>

      <p>
        Hand tempering also forces you to look at the chocolate. You notice when the
        cocoa butter is separating, when the room is too humid, when the beans were
        roasted unevenly. A machine hides all of that behind a dial.
      </p>

      <h2>What to look for at home</h2>
      <p>
        If you are tempering on a countertop, work in a cool room, keep your tools dry,
        and test a smear on parchment. It should set in two or three minutes and pull
        away cleanly. If it stays tacky, the chocolate is too warm.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
